<p>I started coding at age 9. No YouTube. No Stack Overflow. No responsible adult nearby to warn me this would consume the next 40 years of my life.</p>

<p>I'm Sandro Bihaiko, born in Curitiba in 1974 — yes, the same Curitiba with the bi-articulated buses and tube stations from the first post on this blog. I grew up thinking programming was as natural as riding a bike, until I realized most people don't spend weekends debugging BASIC on a computer that runs hotter than a pizza oven.</p>

<p>Self-taught all the way. No degree — I started Electronic Engineering at CEFET, but the market had other plans. Twenty-five years later, I've held pretty much every role this industry offers: developer, analyst, architect, consultant, CTO. I built the internet banking systems for HSBC Brazil and Caixa Econômica Federal. I created JBanana — an open source MVC framework adopted by HSBC and ranked among the top 3 Brazilian open source projects of its time. I wrote for Mundo Java magazine for three years. I've spoken at JustJava, TDC, and software freedom conferences.</p>

<p>I also made games. One won the SXSW Gamer's Voice Best Multiplayer. Another was awarded at LEVEL UY and South Summit Madrid. I built casino games exported to the US, Mexico, and Canada. I created Unity3D tools that ranked in the top 10 best-sellers on the Unity Store. I built a digital whiteboard using a hacked Wii Remote that cost 10x less than the competition — and worked better. The market responded by flooding Brazil with even cheaper Chinese whiteboards. Impeccable timing.</p>

<p>Today I live in Florianópolis, where summer triples the population and paralyzes traffic — giving me plenty of time to write tech articles while waiting for the car in front of me to move.</p>

<p>Currently I'm Co-Founder & CTO at Mutualizo (insurtech) and founder of AIGHA (AI consulting). Before that I spent time at RD Station, where I consolidated 10 years of data into a datalake and left behind — if I do say so myself — a team that actually worked.</p>

<p>This blog is where I put my thoughts on technology, software, AI, and the organized chaos of building things that work. Fair warning: I have strong opinions. Sometimes I'm right. Sometimes I'm not.</p>

<p>That's exactly why this blog is called <strong>Confidently Wrong</strong>.</p>

</Layout>
